# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.drawable).
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT IS FOR. The port has no drawable, and that is the single most expensive
# thing about it: showing one finished frame costs 15.46 ms -- -getBytes: 11.96 ms to pull a
# finished attachment into the process and kRedwoodShow 3.50 ms to push it into the kernel --
# which caps everything at 64.7 frames a second with nothing drawn (docs/metal.md 10.43).
# A drawable removes both copies, because the pass writes into memory the display controller
# already scans out.
#
# -[CAMetalLayer nextDrawable] asks a Metal device exactly one thing for this:
# -newTextureWithDescriptor:iosurface:plane:, a texture over an IOSurface CoreAnimation
# allocated (docs/metal.md 10.46). The library grew rwa_texture_from_surface for it and
# userland/src/redwooddrawable.m is the bridge. NOTHING OF EITHER HAS EVER RUN ON THE PHONE.
# This list is the first time, and it asks the one question that separates a texture over the
# surface's own pages from a texture over a copy of them.
#
# THE QUESTION, AND WHY IT HAS TO BE ASKED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. An import can return a texture
# and still be wrong three ways: over the wrong pages, over the right pages at the wrong
# stride, or over a staging copy that is written back later. In all three the library returns
# a texture and the driver returns an object, so "it worked" means nothing by itself. So
# drawable_test writes through ONE view of the memory and reads through the OTHER:
#
#   1  the processor writes a pattern through IOSurfaceGetBaseAddress, and the texture is read
#      with rwa_texture_read. Equal values => the import found the surface's pages.
#   2  rwa_texture_write puts different values in through the texture, and they are read back
#      through IOSurfaceGetBaseAddress. Equal values => the texture IS those pages. Nothing in
#      this direction writes the surface back, so a staging copy fails here and only here.
#
# The pattern is made from both coordinates, so a wrong stride returns a value that belongs to
# a NAMED other texel instead of merely a wrong value; and the four texels checked are (0,0),
# the end of the first row, the start of the last row and the far corner, because a pitch
# confusion, a height confusion and an offset confusion each show first at one of those.
#
# WHAT IT NEEDS
#
#   gpu=1              in XL_CMDLINE. Without it there is no Adreno to import into, and the
#                      library says so by name; the test then prints NOT MEASURED and stops,
#                      which is not a failure and must not be read as one.
#   an image with GL=1 for /redwood/lib/libredwoodair.dylib, and it must be a library built
#                      AFTER rwa_texture_from_surface existed. An older one is the refusal
#                      "the compiler library in this image has no rwa_texture_from_surface",
#                      and that sentence means the image is stale, not that the path is wrong.
#                      Check it on the machine before booting:
#                        nm -gU xnu-deps/build/mesa/out/libredwoodair.dylib | grep from_surface
#   JOBS=""            not required -- this test shares nothing with the window server -- but
#                      kept for the first boot so that nothing else is allocating surfaces or
#                      GPU buffers while the kernel's table is being read.
#   metal=1            NOT needed. This list does not go through Metal at all: it drives the
#                      bridge and the library directly, in a process that never asks
#                      MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice. That is deliberate -- the conditions
#                      Metal puts on a device object are asserts (docs/metal.md 10.3), so a
#                      boot that measures the import must not also be a boot that can abort
#                      for an unrelated reason.
#   XL_SECS=120        the boot plus two short processes.
#
# WHAT EACH LINE MUST PRINT, AND WHAT WOULD MAKE IT A LIE
#
# 1. THE REFUSALS, which need no GPU and no surface, so they are first and they run on any
#    boot. Six of them, and each must come back with the sentence it is for:
#
#      no device        "a drawable's texture needs the compiler library's device"
#      format 552       the ten-bit XR refusal, which must mention that the kernel substitutes
#                       BGRA -- that is the whole reason it is refused rather than served
#      format 7         "not one this import can lay over a single linear plane", and the
#                       list of what it DOES serve, which the sentence builds from its own
#                       table rather than quoting beside it
#      format 500       must name MTLPixelFormatYCBCR8_420_2P and say TWO PLANES. This is the
#                       one the window server really asked for -- 55 times in the first boot
#                       that carried this file, all for IOSurface 0x104, out of backboardd --
#                       and the refusal is checked for the NAME and for FITTING WHOLE in its
#                       buffer. A reason cut off mid-sentence is a reason nobody can act on
#      format 629/630   the ends of Metal's YCbCr block (500-629): 629 refused with the
#                       block, 630 falling through to the general refusal
#      row 4320         must name 4320 and 64. A refusal that does not quote the number is the
#                       one thing a reader cannot act on
#      row 4352         must NOT be refused: it is what the kernel gives for a 1080-wide BGRA
#                       surface (1080x4 = 4320, aligned up to 4352) and what Adreno takes
#      two planes       "a drawable's texture is one plane"
#
#    WRONG: any of them silent, or 4352 refused. 4352 refused means the agreement between
#    MINISURF_ROW_ALIGN (64) and a6xx's pitchalign (also 64) has broken on one side, and then
#    no drawable can exist until it is found.
/redwood/bin/drawable_test --refusals
#
# 2. THE IMPORT, over a real IOSurface the size of the panel. In order, the log must hold:
#
#    (a) the surface as IOSurface.framework sees it: ID, width, height, bytes a row, alloc
#        size, base address. For 1080x2400 BGRA with no bytes-per-row asked for, the row must
#        be 4352 -- the kernel aligns it to 64 itself (MINISURF_ROW_ALIGN).
#    (b) the same surface as the KERNEL describes it through kRedwoodSurface (0x7e), printed
#        separately on purpose: the two answers come from opposite sides of one call, and
#        either is only worth quoting because the other agrees. The FourCC must read 'BGRA'.
#    (c) the GPU's name, which is the proof the library opened freedreno and not softpipe. If
#        this says softpipe, every line after it is about a rasterizer in plain C and the
#        import will be refused by name -- correctly, and measuring nothing.
#    (d) "a texture over surface N exists".
#    (e) four texels written through the surface and read through the texture, and
#    (f) four texels written through the texture and read through the surface.
#
#    WRONG, and each means a different thing:
#      - the import refused with the pitch sentence: the kernel's stride and a6xx's rule
#        disagree, and the sentence names both numbers.
#      - the import refused with "memory of a process's own": IOSurfaceCreate here wrapped
#        this program's memory, which it was not asked to do. That would be a surprise about
#        IOSurface.framework, not about the GPU.
#      - (e) passes and (f) fails: the import mapped a COPY. The texture reads what was there
#        and writes somewhere else. This is the failure the whole list exists to catch, and it
#        is invisible to any test that reads back through the same path it wrote.
#      - a texel comes back as another texel's value: the two sides disagree about the stride.
#        The value says which texel it belongs to, so the arithmetic is in the log already.
/redwood/bin/drawable_test 1080 2400
#
# 3. THE SAME, SMALL, as a second sample from a second process. 64x64 is one page's worth of
#    rows and a stride of 256 -- already aligned, so it tests the import and not the
#    alignment. Two processes agreeing is worth more than one process repeating: the kernel
#    makes one GPU buffer per user client for a surface, so a second process is a second
#    buffer over the same kind of memory, and a result that holds for only one of them would
#    be about the client and not about the surface.
/redwood/bin/drawable_test 64 64
#
# HOW TO READ THE LOG AFTERWARDS (grep -a: the log has NUL bytes in it)
#
#   grep -a 'drawable_test:' logs/*-run.log
#       everything this list printed, in order.
#   grep -a 'drawable_test: FAILED' logs/*-run.log
#       the only line that must not be there. Nothing at all here and a "0 of N checks
#       failed" line below is the boot this list was written for.
#   grep -a 'drawable_test: .* checks failed' logs/*-run.log
#       three lines, one a process. "0 of" on all three is the answer.
#   grep -a 'drawable_test: NOT MEASURED' logs/*-run.log
#       the honest non-answers: no gpu=1, no library, or a library without the call. Each
#       names which, and none of them is a failure of the path.
#   grep -a 'drawable_test: the kernel says' logs/*-run.log
#       the geometry both sides agreed on, which is the number to quote in any write-up.
#   grep -a 'drawable_test: the GPU is' logs/*-run.log
#       must say an Adreno. "softpipe" here invalidates lines (d) to (f).
#   grep -a 'redwooddrawable:' logs/*-run.log
#       the bridge's own one-off lines: whether the compiler library is loaded and whether it
#       has rwa_texture_from_surface, and whether this process had IOSurfaceGetID.
#   grep -a 'RedwoodGPU: request' logs/*-run.log
#       empty. A line here means the kernel was asked something it does not know, and 0x7e is
#       the request this list depends on.
#   grep -a 'MiniIOSurface' logs/*-run.log
#       the kernel's own complaints about the surface, if any: a format it substituted or a
#       plane count it would not make. Nothing expected for 1080x2400 BGRA.
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT STILL DOES NOT ANSWER, said here so that nobody reads it as more than it is:
#
#   - NO FRAME GOES THROUGH A DRAWABLE. This measures the memory, not the path: there is no
#     CAMetalLayer in it, no nextDrawable, no present, and the driver hook that would make
#     -newTextureWithDescriptor:iosurface:plane: reach this bridge is still only written out
#     in docs/metal.md 10.46 and not applied.
#   - NOTHING IS TIMED. The 15.46 ms the drawable is meant to remove is measured by
#     rc.jobs.metalperf and nothing here replaces that; what a frame costs once a pass writes
#     straight into a surface is unmeasured, and the arithmetic in 10.46 says which part of it
#     is subtraction and which part is a guess.
#   - NOTHING IS DRAWN INTO THE SURFACE BY A SHADER. rwa_texture_write is a transfer, not a
#     render pass, so this says the memory is shared and not that a pass targets it. A pass
#     into an imported surface is the next boot, and it needs the driver hook.
